# `for` builds its block once per entry and keeps each built block (Semantics ch. 4,
# "`for`"), and `@ v` lifts a value into the widget being built: a plain function builds
# nothing, so a `for` is a blueprint there, `@(Nat ==> Nat)`, as a building `match` is
# (`@t`), and not the plain map the signature names. prismc's back end refuses it today
# ("a `for` outside a widget block as a value"); the checker should, as a mismatch.
# Written by Claude on the claude-lowering branch; subject to change.
emit Ui.*
use Ui.*

Doubles: fn [Nat] -> (Nat ==> Nat)
fn Doubles xs = for x <- xs: @ x * 2

@page: @unit
@page:
    Ui.Label($text=$"{Size (Doubles [[1, 2]])}")
